-- title:
--   Saturated Galois-stable submodule realised by a p-divisible group
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $p$ and an intermediate field $K$ of $\mathbb{Q}_p \subseteq \overline{\mathbb{Q}}_p$ (the latter realised as `PadicAlgCl p`) with $K$ finite-dimensional over $\mathbb{Q}_p$, and write $\mathcal{O} =$ [`PadicAlgCl.ringOfIntegers p K`](def/PadicAlgCl_RingOfIntegers.html#L11), the intersection of the integral closure of $\mathbb{Z}_p$ in $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}_p$ with $K$. Let $G$ be a $p$-divisible group of height $h$ over $\mathcal{O}$ in the Hopf-algebraic sense recorded by [`PDivisibleGroup`](def/PDivisibleGroup_Basic.html#L199): levels `G.level v` that are commutative rings, cocommutative Hopf $\mathcal{O}$-algebras, finite and free over $\mathcal{O}$ of rank $p^{vh}$, with surjective bialgebra transition maps `G.transition v : G.level (v+1) → G.level v` whose kernels are the ideals generated by the image of the augmentation ideal under multiplication by $p^v$. Let $M$ be a $\mathbb{Z}_p$-submodule of $T(G) =$ [`TateModule p (G.Points (PadicAlgCl p))`](def/EllipticCurve_TateModule.html#L15), the group of sequences $(x_n)$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}_p$-points of $G$ (the direct limit of the convolution groups of $\mathcal{O}$-algebra homomorphisms `G.level v → PadicAlgCl p`) satisfying $p^n x_n = 0$ and $p x_{n+1} = x_n$. Assume $M$ is stable under the coordinatewise action of every $\mathcal{O}$-algebra automorphism $\tau$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}_p$, and saturated: $r \neq 0$ in $\mathbb{Z}_p$ and $r \cdot x \in M$ imply $x \in M$. Assume given a system $(B_v)_{v \in \mathbb{N}}$ of commutative rings that are cocommutative Hopf $\mathcal{O}$-algebras, finite and free over $\mathcal{O}$, together with surjective bialgebra maps $\pi_v :$ `G.level v` $\to B_v$ and $t_v : B_{v+1} \to B_v$ satisfying $\pi_v \circ$ `G.transition v` $= t_v \circ \pi_{v+1}$, and such that for every $v$ and every point $g$ of $G$ at level $v$ over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}_p$, the algebra homomorphism underlying $g$ factors through $\pi_v$ if and only if the image of $g$ in `G.Points` is the $v$-th coordinate of some $x \in M$. Then there exist a natural number $h'$, a $p$-divisible group $\Gamma$ of height $h'$ over $\mathcal{O}$, a family of bialgebra maps $\varphi_v :$ `G.level v` $\to$ `Γ.level v`, and a $\mathbb{Z}_p$-linear map $T\varphi : T(\Gamma) \to T(G)$ such that: $\varphi_v \circ$ `G.transition v` $=$ `Γ.transition v` $\circ \varphi_{v+1}$ for all $v$; $T\varphi$ is computed levelwise, in the sense that whenever the image in `Γ.Points` of a level-$w$ point $g$ of $\Gamma$ equals the $n$-th coordinate of $x \in T(\Gamma)$, the $n$-th coordinate of $T\varphi(x)$ is the image of the point of $G$ given by the algebra homomorphism $g \circ \varphi_w$; $T\varphi$ commutes with the coordinatewise action of every $\mathcal{O}$-algebra automorphism of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}_p$; and $T\varphi$ is injective with range exactly $M$.
--
--   This is Tate's Proposition 12 in its "sub" form, stated with the closure system $(B_v, \pi_v, t_v)$ of the saturated Galois-stable submodule $M$ taken as a hypothesis rather than constructed: it produces a $p$-divisible group $\Gamma$ over $\mathcal{O}_K$ and a homomorphism $\Gamma \to G$ (given contravariantly by the bialgebra maps $\varphi_v$) identifying $T(\Gamma)$ with $M$ inside $T(G)$. It feeds the uniqueness statement for bialgebra families attached to Galois-equivariant maps on points, and the companion form producing a $p$-divisible group whose Tate module is the kernel of a prescribed quotient.
